  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of ILO constituents and regional and sub-regional constituents and partners (AUC and RECs in particular) to develop and implement labour migration policies and programmes</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Africa, mixed migration flows are increasingly driven by a combination of demographic shifts, technological developments, climate change, environmental pressures, and political factors. This Regional Programme Outcome addresses gaps in labour migration governance and protection at work, which limit the ability of Member States to safeguard the labour rights of migrant workers and refugees and to ensure coherence between migration, employment, skills development, and social protection policies. During the biennium, the programme supports Member States to establish, strengthen, and operationalize labour migration frameworks and institutional mechanisms aligned with international labour standards and regional commitments. Expected results include an increased number of countries with functional policy and institutional arrangements to protect labour rights; the introduction or improvement of services for migrant workers and refugees; and strengthened bilateral and regional labour migration frameworks with monitoring and review mechanisms.
The primary beneficiaries are Member State institutions responsible for labour, employment, migration, and social protection, as well as employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations engaged in labour migration governance. Migrant workers and refugees benefit indirectly through improved access to rights-based services, safer labour mobility, better working conditions, and stronger protection mechanisms across countries of origin, transit, and destination.</narrative>
  </description>
